    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d725">

                <pb facs="0646" n="637" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg637"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/4176: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Leopoldville</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Mallon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Leopoldville</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-06-25T16:00:00+01:00">June 25, 1940—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 26—6:26 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>In reply to the Department’s telegram of June 24<anchor n="14" xml:id="d725-f1"/>
                    French military and civil authorities of French Equatorial Africa nearly
                    unanimous in <pb facs="0647" n="638" xml:id="pg638"/> desire to continue war.
                    The Governor General has informed the British Consul General at Leopoldville in
                    strict confidence that he has telegraphed today all other French Governors in
                    Africa that he is prepared to join in a solid bloc of African colonies to carry
                    on the war regardless of any action taken by the French Government. Many French
                    officers and civilians have expressed a desire of joining the British armed
                    forces should the Colonial Government capitulate.</p>
                <p>The Governor General of the Belgian Congo announced today that the Belgian
                    Government has conferred on him full powers formerly vested in the King and
                    Colonial Council and that he will give full support to the British in continuing
                    the fight.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mallon</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
